const course = JSON.parse("{\"id\":\"quintus-horatius-flaccus\",\"slug\":\"quintus-horatius-flaccus\",\"title\":\"Quintus Horatius Flaccus\",\"subtitle\":\"Horace: life, poetry and the Augustan world\",\"description\":\"A renowned poet, critic, and translator during the Augustan period, known for works such as Ars Poetica. He is one of the representatives of the Golden Age of Roman literature. This course leads you deep into his life and works.\",\"category\":\"European Humanities\",\"categoryId\":\"european-humanities\",\"subjectId\":\"poetry\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tags\":[\"Poetry\"],\"cover\":\"https://learningguide-1380131816.cos.ap-hongkong.myqcloud.com/mvp/image/Horatius.jpg\",\"thumbnailPath\":\"https://learningguide-1380131816.cos.ap-hongkong.myqcloud.com/mvp/image/Horatius.jpg\",\"referencePrice\":29.99,\"discount\":0.7,\"authorIds\":[],\"status\":\"published\",\"sections\":[{\"id\":\"horatius-section-1\",\"title\":\"Section 1\",\"lessons\":[{\"id\":\"horatius-lesson-21\",\"title\":\"Life of Horace\",\"body\":\"The legendary life of Horace from soldier to poet laureate\",\"durationMinutes\":20,\"videoDurationSeconds\":null,\"isPublic\":true,\"contents\":[{\"id\":\"content-21-video\",\"title\":\"Life of Horace\",\"type\":\"video\",\"mode\":\"lecture\",\"url\":\"https://learningguide-1380131816.cos.ap-hongkong.myqcloud.com/mvp/video/%E8%B4%BA%E6%8B%89%E6%96%AF%E8%AF%97%E6%AD%8C%E4%B8%BA%E4%BD%95%E5%80%BC%E5%BE%97%E4%B8%80%E8%AF%BB.mp4\",\"nodes\":[]}]}]},{\"id\":\"horatius-section-2\",\"title\":\"Section 2\",\"lessons\":[{\"id\":\"horatius-lesson-22\",\"title\":\"Odes Appreciation\",\"body\":\"<p><strong>A Low-Energy Poet</strong></p>\\n<h1><strong><em>Introduction</em></strong></h1>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (1).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 1&gt;</span>In Europe up until the end of\\n  the\\n  19th\\n  century, being educated implied an acquaintance with the Classics, that is Latin and Ancient Greek. In the 21st\\n  century a pupil who studies Classics is a <em>rara avis</em>. Most schools do not have qualified teachers. Admittedly,\\n  you can still study Classics at university, but only because a knowledge of Latin and Greek is no longer required. You\\n  can study the literature, history and philosophy in translation and learn the two languages at the same time. As\\n  Classics has moved from the centre of Western education and culture to the margins, so there has also been a change in\\n  the classical texts that are studied and talked about. The preference for Greek over Latin authors, which emerged in\\n  the 19<sup>th</sup> century, has become more marked. Silicon Valley talks endlessly about Homer, but there is much\\n  less discussion of Virgil; to have read Thucydides is the mark of a deep thinker, but no one boasts about reading\\n  Livy. On top of this, within Latin itself, there is less focus on the golden age of Latin literature, the Augustan\\n  era. These are well-fished waters; and academics in search of new things to say cast their nets elsewhere. One poet\\n  whose reputation has suffered from both these trends is Horace. In the Middle Ages he was the most quoted classical\\n  author, but today he is much less read. </p>\\n<p>Horace’s poetry itself is also to blame for this fall in popularity. All poetry is difficult to translate, but Horace\\n  loses more than most poets in translation. Unfortunately, to appreciate that, you need to have a good feeling for\\n  Latin.<span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (2).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 2&gt;</span> To take a simple example, in\\n  one poem he\\n  describes a previous lover, Pyrrha, as “<em>simplex munditiis</em>”. The word “<em>simplex</em>” is the English word\\n  “simple” and it contains two ideas: a lack of complexity or plainness and a lack of guile or honesty.\\n  “<em>Munditiis</em>” means the action carried out to make something presentable. It can be used, for example, to\\n  describe tidying and cleaning up a house. But it can also mean the result of that activity, the condition of a house\\n  that is now clean and tidy. When applied to a woman it refers either to what she has done to make herself attractive,\\n  e.g. how she has done her hair or the clothes she wears, or to the effect of those choices, that she now looks stylish\\n  and elegant. So the most obvious meaning of “<em>simplex munditiis</em>” is that Pyrrha has a natural elegance to her,\\n  without having to rely on make-up or expensive jewellery or clothes. However, a little more thought reveals that these\\n  two words are uneasy bed-fellows. If you tidy up your house before your guests arrive for dinner, the house your\\n  guests see misrepresents what your house normally looks like. The phrase “<em>simplex munditiis</em>” hints at what\\n  the rest of poem makes clear: with Pyrrha, what you see is definitely not what you get. Her natural elegance is the\\n  result of considerable effort and skill to present herself in that way; and she is therefore not at all simple in the\\n  sense of straightforward or ingenuous. I remember a few years back being on a flight to Nanjing. I was sitting in the\\n  window seat and a young lady sat in the aisle seat of the same row. I was writing an email at the time and had the\\n  impression that she was casually dressed and carrying a large, nondescript bag. It was only when I finished writing\\n  and looked up that I noticed that her jacket and trousers were tailored and the bag had a discretely located and\\n  expensive logo.<em> Simplex munditiis. </em>I was not surprised when she later spoke to me in good, American-accented\\n  English. This two-word phrase is also a good description of Horace’s poetry itself: on the surface it appears simple,\\n  because his vocabulary is mostly that of everyday speech, but his careful choice and combination of words lend these\\n  ordinary words a marvellous expressiveness. </p>\\n<p>A second difficulty is the background knowledge of Roman history and culture needed to understand him. Of course,\\n  that is a problem with any work written in a foreign language. I enjoy reading (in English ) Jin Yong and I think\\n  that, provided you’ve seen a few Hong Kong kung fu films, you can understand most of what is happening, without\\n  knowing the details about Chinese medicine or the Jin dynasty. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (3).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 3&gt;</span>Horace however makes more\\n  demands than\\n  most poets; for he assumes the reader knows many\\n  details about daily life in the Roman world. Not only do you need to recognise the names Cleopatra and Cyprus, but\\n  also Bibulus and Bandusia. With him, you need to spend more time looking things up.</p>\\n<p>Another problem is that his values are out of tune with those of the modern Western reader. His outlook is\\n  unabashedly conservative, even petit bourgeois. He respected and ingratiated himself with Rome's elites and his poetry\\n  was literary propaganda for the Augustan principate. Whereas Juvenal’s satires are directed at the powerful and\\n  famous, Horace’s satires punch down: he makes fun of prostitutes, the uneducated and foreigners.<span\\n    class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (4).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 4&gt;</span> In fact, if one line of his\\n  poetry\\n  might be recognised today, it is because it became the\\n  title of a famous poem by a modern English poet, Owen: ”<em>Dulce et decorum est</em>&quot;. What is it that Horace\\n  said is sweet and seemly (<em>dulce et decorum</em>)? To die for your country. Owen called this &quot;the old\\n  lie&quot; and his poem is a description of the reality of fighting in the trenches in World War I, which was anything\\n  but “sweet and seemly”. So, if Horace is known today, he is most likely to be known as the representative of an\\n  ignorant, out-dated and dangerous attitude towards war. Such an assessment would be unfounded and unfair. Horace, like\\n  Owen, had first-hand experience of the horrors of war and his support for Augustus was motivated by his revulsion at\\n  the carnage during the civil wars of the late Roman Republic. Nevertheless, there is something to the criticism; and\\n  Horace has had admirers and detractors from Roman times though the Middle Ages until today. He is no friend of the\\n  radical, who sees the imperfections of the world and is driven to change them. He is a realist, who sees the same\\n  imperfections and counsels accommodation to them.</p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (5).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 5&gt;</span>Finally, connected with the\\n  point\\n  above, there are the topics he writes about\\n  and the perspective he writes about them from. He is not writing about heroes from mythology or Roman history or about\\n  tragic love affairs or the mysteries of the universe. In his most famous work, the Odes, he is for the most part\\n  writing about mundane things, like enjoying a glass of wine or inviting your boss to dinner or the attractions of a\\n  local beauty spot. Sometimes he will write about more newsworthy events, such as the suicide of Cleopatra, but his\\n  perspective is always that of an ordinary person. Horace was well-connected with Roman elite society - his patron was\\n  the third most powerful person in Rome - but the odes seem to have been written by someone who was an NPC. Even when\\n  he is writing love poetry, he is at one remove from the action: he is writing about someone else's affairs or, if they\\n  are his own affairs, he is remembering them from the past. His tone tends to be objective and moderate rather than\\n  dramatic and emotional. The one deeper and more dramatic theme that reoccurs in his poems is the depressing one of\\n  death. In short, his poetry is not that of a participant in what he writes about, but of a journalist, reporting on\\n  it, and of a neurotic one at that. If Trump were to become a classical literary critic, he would certainly accuse\\n  Horace of being low-energy.</p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (6).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 6&gt;</span>So, why read Horace, then?\\n  Well,\\n  mainly because he’s a great poet. His mastery\\n  of the Latin language, the evocativeness of his imagery and the mercurial turns in his thinking mean it is possible to\\n  reread his poems and find something new and important in them each time you do. But I feel that what makes him\\n  unpopular today also makes him worth reading today. He is a vaccine against some of the cultural viruses of today’s\\n  world: the TikTok attention spans, the extremes of following your dream or lying flat and the relentless appeals to\\n  emotion and instinct. Reading Horace means you’ll need to spend time consulting a commentary to understand what he\\n  says. You’ll be confronted by a philosophy that tries to chart a middle ground between changing what you can and\\n  accepting what you can’t. You will need to read between the lines of someone who always seems to be at one remove from\\n  whatever he is describing and to pay attention to the change of scene, perspective or tone in a poem. His poetry\\n  teaches you to spend some time figuring out what’s going on before rushing to an emotional response. </p>\\n<p>As a sampler of his poetry, here is one of his more famous odes, one which makes fewer demands on understanding Roman\\n  cultural or historical references, together with some analysis. Don’t take the analysis too seriously. For one thing,\\n  I am no expert in classics or poetry. The analysis is just a collection of what more knowledgeable people have said\\n  about the poem or, more accurately, it’s my understanding of the things more knowledgeable people have said and I’m\\n  sure I’ve misunderstood some of them. But there’s a more important reason for not taking my analysis seriously. The\\n  analysis is only a guidebook. Now, suppose you are on holiday in a foreign country and you find in your guidebook a\\n  description of one of its tourist attractions, say a beautiful, stately house or the panoramic view from a mountain\\n  top. Who would be satisfied with just reading the description, no matter how good, when, with just a little effort,\\n  you can go there, walk around and see it for yourself? Here’s the translation. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\"\\n    data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\" data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (7).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 7&gt;</span>\\n</p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Soracte Ode</em></strong></p>\\n<p> Can you see how, glittering white in deep snow, </p>\\n<p>Vides ut alta stet nive candidum </p>\\n<p>Soracte stands there? How the labouring forests</p>\\n<p>Soracte nec iam sustineant onus</p>\\n<p>can no longer bear their load and the rivers</p>\\n<p>silvae laborantes geluque</p>\\n<p>are frozen solid with sharp ice?</p>\\n<p>flumina constiterint acuto?</p>\\n<p>Melt the cold by laying large heaps </p>\\n<p>Dissolve frigus ligna super foco </p>\\n<p>of firewood on the hearth. And be more generous</p>\\n<p>large reponens atque benignius</p>\\n<p>in bringing out the four-year old wine </p>\\n<p>deprome quadrimum Sabina,</p>\\n<p>Thaliarchus, in the Sabine two-handled wine-jar.</p>\\n<p>O Thaliarche, merum diota.</p>\\n<p>Leave the rest to the gods. Once they</p>\\n<p>Permitte divis cetera, qui simul </p>\\n<p>have calmed the winds battling it out</p>\\n<p>strauere ventos aequore fervido</p>\\n<p>on the turbulent sea, the cypresses</p>\\n<p>deproeliantis, nec cupressi</p>\\n<p>and the old ash trees will also be at peace.</p>\\n<p>nec veteres agitantur orni.</p>\\n<p>Stop investigating what will happen tomorrow, and</p>\\n<p>Quid sit futurum cras, fuge quaerere, et </p>\\n<p>whatever sort of day fortune gives, record it</p>\\n<p>quem fors dierum cumque dabit, lucro</p>\\n<p>as a windfall profit. Don’t dismiss </p>\\n<p>adpone nec dulcis amores </p>\\n<p>the delights of love or the dance-floor,</p>\\n<p>sperne, puer, neque tu choreas, </p>\\n<p>while you are young and you have no crabbed </p>\\n<p>donec virenti canities abest </p>\\n<p>white hairs. Seek out now at the agreed time </p>\\n<p>morosa. Nunc et Campus et areae</p>\\n<p>the main square and the piazzas </p>\\n<p>lenesque sub noctem susurri </p>\\n<p>and the gentle nighttime whispers.</p>\\n<p>composita repetantur hora,</p>\\n<p>Now is also the time for the girl’s pleasing laughter</p>\\n<p>nunc et latentis proditor intimo </p>\\n<p>from a distant corner, betraying where’s she’s hidden, </p>\\n<p>gratus puellae risus ab angulo</p>\\n<p>and a love-token, pulled off a forearm, </p>\\n<p>pignusque dereptum lacertis</p>\\n<p>or off a barely resisting finger. </p>\\n<p>Aut digito male pertinaci.</p>\\n<p><strong><em>First Thoughts</em></strong></p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (8).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 8&gt;</span>To begin with, some\\n  explanations\\n  and comments on the translation. In the first\\n  stanza, Soracte is a mountain in Italy. Horace describes the trees covered by a blanket of snow as if they were\\n  porters, struggling and failing to carry too heavy a load. So one visual image that comes to mind is of tree branches,\\n  overloaded with snow, breaking and falling to the ground; and this is what one Chinese translation of the poem says.\\n  But it is not what Horace says; and I think this sort of translation is doing some of the work that the reader is\\n  supposed to do. In general, I have restricted the Chinese to what Horace actually says. On another translation point,\\n  I have tried not to smooth over the strangeness of what Horace says. Sometimes the strangeness reflects the fact that\\n  Horace’s world is different from today’s. This fact is worth noticing and the strangeness brings these differences to\\n  your attention. Sometimes Horace’s language is deliberately strange. My Chinese teacher will tell me that in Chinese\\n  trees can’t feel tired, and she’s right. But in Latin trees can’t feel tired either, yet that’s what Horace says, so\\n  I’ve kept the strangeness of the language in Chinese.</p>\\n<p> </p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (9).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 9&gt;</span>In the second stanza, the name\\n  Thaliarchus is Greek. When a group of friends\\n  meet for drinks, the thaliarch is the person in charge of the drinking. Sabina is a region in northern Italy. Its wine\\n  was not the equivalent of an expensive French variety, more the sort of low-priced wine you’d buy at the local\\n  supermarket. Horace was a proponent of the simple life and he was disdainful of imported, foreign luxuries. Now, a\\n  general point here: when I say “Horace”, I usually mean Horace the poet, i.e. the picture of him that emerges from\\n  what he says in his poetry. What the real, historical Horace believed, it is difficult to say with certainty. What\\n  Horace the poet says was public speech for Horace the real person, so the relationship between the two Horaces is\\n  complicated. The word for wine jar is a Greek word, meaning two-eared, which refers to the two handles for carrying\\n  the jar. It stands out and sounds sophisticated, like when someone inserts a French word into a passage in English.\\n</p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (10).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 10&gt;</span>In the next two stanza,\\n  Horace\\n  gives some general and some specific advice to\\n  Thaliarchus. The darkest hour comes before the dawn; the future is unpredictable; count your blessings. While you’re\\n  still young, don’t miss out on romance and dancing. Horace’s advice uses accounting terminology: if you were to record\\n  what each day brings as you do household financial transactions, you would see that the gains outweigh the losses. It\\n  is not quite clear what dancing refers to. It is not couples dancing. Nor is it the dancing of flute girls, the\\n  ancient world’s version of exotic or pole dancing; for that did not take place outdoors in public spaces. Most likely\\n  it is a group of men doing formation dancing. White hairs are crabbed because people fret about getting white hairs,\\n  but also because white hairs are a sign of old age, and old people are crabbed. </p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (11).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 11&gt;</span>The last two stanzas present\\n  a\\n  love scene. The Campus Martius was the main\\n  square in Rome. A young man is chasing a young girl. She finds a hiding place to escape from him, but laughs when he\\n  cannot find her. This reveals her hiding place, and he manages to snatch a love token from her, either a bracelet or a\\n  ring. Up until now, what Horace has been saying has been pretty straightforward, but now words start to have more than\\n  one meaning and the translation falls short. The laughter is “gratus” because it is a lovely sound, but also something\\n  to be grateful for, because it reveals where the girl is hiding; the corner is “intimo” because it is far away from\\n  anywhere else, but also because it brings the lovers close together; and the finger is “male pertinaci” because it\\n  barely resists and mischievously pretends to resist. </p>\\n<p>With this background, read the translation through a few times more. Write down your first impressions. What is the\\n  poem about? How does the narrative progress and how does the emotional tone change during the poem? Once you’ve\\n  written down your ideas, then proceed with the rest of the introduction. </p>\\n<p>It is easy to see what is attractive about the poem. First, there are the three marvellously described scenes –\\n  gazing at the countryside in winter, drinking wine by the fire and playing hide and seek with a pretty girl. Every\\n  detail of the three scenes is carefully chosen. Despite the gap of two millenia, Horace is describing sights and\\n  experiences that everyone today recognises. Snow still sits on tree branches in winter; it still feels comfortable to\\n  be dry indoors while the storm beats the rain against the window outside; and a romantic evening is still about\\n  chasing someone who wants to be found, even if love-tokens are no longer part of the modern dating scene. However, the\\n  poem also seems to contain some less successful elements. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (12).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 12&gt;</span>It starts with a\\n  cold day in the countryside, shifts indoors into a warm house and then moves outside again to a pleasant Spring or\\n  Summer evening in the city. That’s a lot of costume changes for one poem! What’s the logic behind them? The imagery,\\n  too, seems contradictory. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (13).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh\\n    13&gt;</span>Consider the two images Horace introduces to support\\n  his advice to leave things to the gods – the storm at sea and on the land. First, as the commentators have pointed\\n  out, there is the mismatch with the winter scene in the opening stanza. Both scenes symbolise unpleasant situations.\\n  But in the first everything is stationary – the mountain, the trees and the rivers ; in the second the wind is\\n  buffeting the trees and churning the sea. Secondly, there is the change from land to sea and back to land again. While\\n  each scene is successful on its own, it’s not clear how they fit together. </p>\\n<p>Another complaint relates to Horace’s advice. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (14).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh\\n    14&gt;</span> For starters, a modern reader\\n  finds it strange that a poem contains such explicit moralising. Surely a poem’s message is something that is implied\\n  or hinted at? But Horace just comes out and tells you what to think. Not only that, but some of the advice, given a\\n  moment’s thought, sounds naive. Yes, it makes sense to stop worrying about things in the future you have no control\\n  over and just enjoy the things you can enjoy now. Perhaps it’s also true that just as no good experience can last\\n  forever, neither can any bad experience. But who thinks that every day is a good day? Who thinks the day you get fired\\n  from your job or, to take a Roman example, the day your family farm is expropriated and given to a legionary veteran\\n  is a day where the pluses outweigh the minuses? And yet this is what Horace us saying. A final complaint, less\\n  remarked on by the commentators, is the weakness of the ending. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (15).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 15&gt;</span>The final\\n  stanza describes a sequence of episodes in a romantic encounter and then just stops at an arbitrary point. As result,\\n  the poem’s ending seems to be incomplete and missing something. This inconclusiveness is only made worse by there\\n  being two options for what happened and what was snatched from where. The poem doesn’t so much come to a conclusion as\\n  stumble off with a question mark.</p>\\n<p>Are these justified criticisms of what is otherwise a very beautiful poem? The greatest classical scholar of the\\n  19<sup>th</sup> century and the greatest classical scholar of the 20<sup>th</sup> century thought so. They judged it a\\n  flawed jewel. They admired its language and imagery, but thought that the poem doesn’t form a coherent whole. Now I’m\\n  sure their understanding of Horace and literary taste is much better than mine ( or yours ), so the prosecution’s case\\n  is a strong one. However, the defence can call an expert witness to refute this assessment: Horace himself. He chose\\n  to publish the Soracte Ode in the collection of poems he claimed would win him poetic immortality, so he was obviously\\n  satisfied with it; and his judgement of Latin poetry must be superior to any scholar’s. Given this, it is worth\\n  investigating further whether these complaints are really flaws or just misunderstandings of Horace’s poetic technique\\n  or intentions. </p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Lens of Literary Tradition </em></strong></p>\\n<p>One approach to figuring out Horace’s intentions is to investigate the literary tradition he was working in. A modern\\n  poet is expected to create something unique and original based on personal experience; calling a work derivative is to\\n  assess it as second rate; and plagiarism is unethical behaviour. But this expectation is a product of the Romantic\\n  revolution in Western literature, bolstered more recently, no doubt, by the commercial considerations of copyright\\n  law. The Romans had neither. Cicero wrote all five books of his treatise on ethics, On Moral Ends, in a little over\\n  one month. He could only do this because he took all the content, often word for word, from Greek philosophical\\n  handbooks and translated it into Latin. One of Catullus’ most famous poems is largely a translation from a Greek poem\\n  by Sappho. The major literary revolution in the classical world was the Hellenistic one, which marked the transition\\n  of Greece from an oral to a written culture. One of its consequences was to tie poetry more explicitly to the poetic\\n  tradition. A feature of Hellenistic poetry was deliberate and erudite allusions to older poets, which the educated\\n  reader was expected to recognise and appreciate. So it is wrong to expect Horace’s odes to be a freeform composition,\\n  emerging from his life experiences. </p>\\n<p>So what sort of poetry was Horace’s odes? <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (16).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh\\n    16&gt;</span>To answer this question, let’s\\n  return to the example used earlier – the Westerner reading a Jin Yong novel – but make it a bit more complicated – a\\n  Westerner watching an episode of Wulin Waizhuan. Now, apart from having better Chinese than mine, what would sort of\\n  knowledge would be needed to get the jokes? I would suggest two things. First, knowledge of the world depicted in\\n  Chinese martial arts literature, not just its typical characters and plots, but also the beliefs, customs and social\\n  structure of the era the action takes place in, namely the Ming Dynasty. Secondly, a knowledge of the modern world and\\n  how it works. A lot of the humour comes from the interplay between these two worlds, for example imagining advertising\\n  and TV quiz shows in Ming Dynasty China. A similar sort of interplay is central to Horace’s odes as well; for lyric\\n  poetry in Latin did not exist before Horace and his challenge in inventing it was to take the metres and poetic\\n  conventions from the Greek tradition and not just turn them into Latin, but make them say something relevant to the\\n  Roman world. This means that someone reading Horace today has a more difficult job than the Westerner watching My Own\\n  Swordsman. The latter has already acquired half of the algorithm needed to make sense of an episode: modern life in\\n  any big Western city is not so different from life in any big Chinese city. But the modern reader of Horace lacks\\n  knowledge not only of Greek lyric poetry but also of life in Rome in the late Republic and early Principate period.\\n</p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Lens of Lyric Poetry</em></strong></p>\\n<p>Even if you don’t understand much about Chinese martial arts literature or modern-day China, you can still enjoy\\n  watching an episode of Wulin Waizhuan. It is a comedy TV series; and all comedy depends on funny characters, funny\\n  situations and funny language. Another way to approach the Soracte ode is to treat it not as Latin poetry, but simply\\n  as poetry. Poetry conveys ideas and feelings by using certain techniques, such as a careful selection of words, metre\\n  and imagery. It is your job as the reader to be sensitive to these features of a poem; for they will help you towards\\n  a better understanding and appreciation. </p>\\n<p>One aspect of a poem’s words is their sounds. Poetry relies, much more than prose, on the sound of words to convey\\n  meaning. Reading today is a silent and solitary activity, but this is a modern practice and it assumes the\\n  availability of mass-produced books. Roman poetry was written to be read aloud to an audience. Read the Latin version\\n  of Horace’s poem out loud; or, if you can’t read Latin, here’s a recording. </p>\\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"6\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"https://learningguide-1380131816.cos.ap-hongkong.myqcloud.com/mvp/other/Horace%20Recording.m4a\\\">\\n    &lt;Exh 17&gt;\\n  </span></p>\\n<p>\\n  Listen to the sounds of the words and notice the effect they have on you, even if you don’t know what the words mean.\\n  Now listen again to the first stanza, which talks about the trees struggling under the weight of the snow on their\\n  branches and the rivers turned into blocks of ice.\\n</p>\\n<p>\\n  How do the sounds of the Latin contribute to Horace’s description?\\n</p>\\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\\n  <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"3\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"How do the sounds of the Latin contribute to Horace’s description?\\\"\\n    data-instance-answer-type=\\\"input\\\"\\n    data-instance-answer-result=\\\"Can you hear the trees gasping, groaning and grunting under their burden in the sentence “nec iam sustineant onus/ silvae laborantes”? All those “S”, “M”, “N” and “T” sounds. Or in the last line “flumina constiterint acuto”, can you hear how the flowing sound of the Latin word for “river” (flumina) is followed by the repeated “C” and “T” sounds of words for “congeal” and “sharp” (constiterint and acuto). Compare this with the softness of the sounds in the line towards the end of the poem that describes the lovers’ nighttime meetings “lenesque sub noctem susurri”.\\\">\\n    Answer the question\\n  </span>\\n</p>\",\"durationMinutes\":45,\"isPublic\":true,\"contents\":[{\"id\":\"content-22-text\",\"title\":\"Odes Appreciation\",\"type\":\"text\",\"mode\":\"lecture\",\"html\":\"<p><strong>A Low-Energy Poet</strong></p>\\n<h1><strong><em>Introduction</em></strong></h1>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (1).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 1&gt;</span>In Europe up until the end of\\n  the\\n  19th\\n  century, being educated implied an acquaintance with the Classics, that is Latin and Ancient Greek. In the 21st\\n  century a pupil who studies Classics is a <em>rara avis</em>. Most schools do not have qualified teachers. Admittedly,\\n  you can still study Classics at university, but only because a knowledge of Latin and Greek is no longer required. You\\n  can study the literature, history and philosophy in translation and learn the two languages at the same time. As\\n  Classics has moved from the centre of Western education and culture to the margins, so there has also been a change in\\n  the classical texts that are studied and talked about. The preference for Greek over Latin authors, which emerged in\\n  the 19<sup>th</sup> century, has become more marked. Silicon Valley talks endlessly about Homer, but there is much\\n  less discussion of Virgil; to have read Thucydides is the mark of a deep thinker, but no one boasts about reading\\n  Livy. On top of this, within Latin itself, there is less focus on the golden age of Latin literature, the Augustan\\n  era. These are well-fished waters; and academics in search of new things to say cast their nets elsewhere. One poet\\n  whose reputation has suffered from both these trends is Horace. In the Middle Ages he was the most quoted classical\\n  author, but today he is much less read. </p>\\n<p>Horace’s poetry itself is also to blame for this fall in popularity. All poetry is difficult to translate, but Horace\\n  loses more than most poets in translation. Unfortunately, to appreciate that, you need to have a good feeling for\\n  Latin.<span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (2).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 2&gt;</span> To take a simple example, in\\n  one poem he\\n  describes a previous lover, Pyrrha, as “<em>simplex munditiis</em>”. The word “<em>simplex</em>” is the English word\\n  “simple” and it contains two ideas: a lack of complexity or plainness and a lack of guile or honesty.\\n  “<em>Munditiis</em>” means the action carried out to make something presentable. It can be used, for example, to\\n  describe tidying and cleaning up a house. But it can also mean the result of that activity, the condition of a house\\n  that is now clean and tidy. When applied to a woman it refers either to what she has done to make herself attractive,\\n  e.g. how she has done her hair or the clothes she wears, or to the effect of those choices, that she now looks stylish\\n  and elegant. So the most obvious meaning of “<em>simplex munditiis</em>” is that Pyrrha has a natural elegance to her,\\n  without having to rely on make-up or expensive jewellery or clothes. However, a little more thought reveals that these\\n  two words are uneasy bed-fellows. If you tidy up your house before your guests arrive for dinner, the house your\\n  guests see misrepresents what your house normally looks like. The phrase “<em>simplex munditiis</em>” hints at what\\n  the rest of poem makes clear: with Pyrrha, what you see is definitely not what you get. Her natural elegance is the\\n  result of considerable effort and skill to present herself in that way; and she is therefore not at all simple in the\\n  sense of straightforward or ingenuous. I remember a few years back being on a flight to Nanjing. I was sitting in the\\n  window seat and a young lady sat in the aisle seat of the same row. I was writing an email at the time and had the\\n  impression that she was casually dressed and carrying a large, nondescript bag. It was only when I finished writing\\n  and looked up that I noticed that her jacket and trousers were tailored and the bag had a discretely located and\\n  expensive logo.<em> Simplex munditiis. </em>I was not surprised when she later spoke to me in good, American-accented\\n  English. This two-word phrase is also a good description of Horace’s poetry itself: on the surface it appears simple,\\n  because his vocabulary is mostly that of everyday speech, but his careful choice and combination of words lend these\\n  ordinary words a marvellous expressiveness. </p>\\n<p>A second difficulty is the background knowledge of Roman history and culture needed to understand him. Of course,\\n  that is a problem with any work written in a foreign language. I enjoy reading (in English ) Jin Yong and I think\\n  that, provided you’ve seen a few Hong Kong kung fu films, you can understand most of what is happening, without\\n  knowing the details about Chinese medicine or the Jin dynasty. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (3).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 3&gt;</span>Horace however makes more\\n  demands than\\n  most poets; for he assumes the reader knows many\\n  details about daily life in the Roman world. Not only do you need to recognise the names Cleopatra and Cyprus, but\\n  also Bibulus and Bandusia. With him, you need to spend more time looking things up.</p>\\n<p>Another problem is that his values are out of tune with those of the modern Western reader. His outlook is\\n  unabashedly conservative, even petit bourgeois. He respected and ingratiated himself with Rome's elites and his poetry\\n  was literary propaganda for the Augustan principate. Whereas Juvenal’s satires are directed at the powerful and\\n  famous, Horace’s satires punch down: he makes fun of prostitutes, the uneducated and foreigners.<span\\n    class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (4).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 4&gt;</span> In fact, if one line of his\\n  poetry\\n  might be recognised today, it is because it became the\\n  title of a famous poem by a modern English poet, Owen: ”<em>Dulce et decorum est</em>&quot;. What is it that Horace\\n  said is sweet and seemly (<em>dulce et decorum</em>)? To die for your country. Owen called this &quot;the old\\n  lie&quot; and his poem is a description of the reality of fighting in the trenches in World War I, which was anything\\n  but “sweet and seemly”. So, if Horace is known today, he is most likely to be known as the representative of an\\n  ignorant, out-dated and dangerous attitude towards war. Such an assessment would be unfounded and unfair. Horace, like\\n  Owen, had first-hand experience of the horrors of war and his support for Augustus was motivated by his revulsion at\\n  the carnage during the civil wars of the late Roman Republic. Nevertheless, there is something to the criticism; and\\n  Horace has had admirers and detractors from Roman times though the Middle Ages until today. He is no friend of the\\n  radical, who sees the imperfections of the world and is driven to change them. He is a realist, who sees the same\\n  imperfections and counsels accommodation to them.</p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (5).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 5&gt;</span>Finally, connected with the\\n  point\\n  above, there are the topics he writes about\\n  and the perspective he writes about them from. He is not writing about heroes from mythology or Roman history or about\\n  tragic love affairs or the mysteries of the universe. In his most famous work, the Odes, he is for the most part\\n  writing about mundane things, like enjoying a glass of wine or inviting your boss to dinner or the attractions of a\\n  local beauty spot. Sometimes he will write about more newsworthy events, such as the suicide of Cleopatra, but his\\n  perspective is always that of an ordinary person. Horace was well-connected with Roman elite society - his patron was\\n  the third most powerful person in Rome - but the odes seem to have been written by someone who was an NPC. Even when\\n  he is writing love poetry, he is at one remove from the action: he is writing about someone else's affairs or, if they\\n  are his own affairs, he is remembering them from the past. His tone tends to be objective and moderate rather than\\n  dramatic and emotional. The one deeper and more dramatic theme that reoccurs in his poems is the depressing one of\\n  death. In short, his poetry is not that of a participant in what he writes about, but of a journalist, reporting on\\n  it, and of a neurotic one at that. If Trump were to become a classical literary critic, he would certainly accuse\\n  Horace of being low-energy.</p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (6).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 6&gt;</span>So, why read Horace, then?\\n  Well,\\n  mainly because he’s a great poet. His mastery\\n  of the Latin language, the evocativeness of his imagery and the mercurial turns in his thinking mean it is possible to\\n  reread his poems and find something new and important in them each time you do. But I feel that what makes him\\n  unpopular today also makes him worth reading today. He is a vaccine against some of the cultural viruses of today’s\\n  world: the TikTok attention spans, the extremes of following your dream or lying flat and the relentless appeals to\\n  emotion and instinct. Reading Horace means you’ll need to spend time consulting a commentary to understand what he\\n  says. You’ll be confronted by a philosophy that tries to chart a middle ground between changing what you can and\\n  accepting what you can’t. You will need to read between the lines of someone who always seems to be at one remove from\\n  whatever he is describing and to pay attention to the change of scene, perspective or tone in a poem. His poetry\\n  teaches you to spend some time figuring out what’s going on before rushing to an emotional response. </p>\\n<p>As a sampler of his poetry, here is one of his more famous odes, one which makes fewer demands on understanding Roman\\n  cultural or historical references, together with some analysis. Don’t take the analysis too seriously. For one thing,\\n  I am no expert in classics or poetry. The analysis is just a collection of what more knowledgeable people have said\\n  about the poem or, more accurately, it’s my understanding of the things more knowledgeable people have said and I’m\\n  sure I’ve misunderstood some of them. But there’s a more important reason for not taking my analysis seriously. The\\n  analysis is only a guidebook. Now, suppose you are on holiday in a foreign country and you find in your guidebook a\\n  description of one of its tourist attractions, say a beautiful, stately house or the panoramic view from a mountain\\n  top. Who would be satisfied with just reading the description, no matter how good, when, with just a little effort,\\n  you can go there, walk around and see it for yourself? Here’s the translation. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\"\\n    data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\" data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (7).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 7&gt;</span>\\n</p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Soracte Ode</em></strong></p>\\n<p> Can you see how, glittering white in deep snow, </p>\\n<p>Vides ut alta stet nive candidum </p>\\n<p>Soracte stands there? How the labouring forests</p>\\n<p>Soracte nec iam sustineant onus</p>\\n<p>can no longer bear their load and the rivers</p>\\n<p>silvae laborantes geluque</p>\\n<p>are frozen solid with sharp ice?</p>\\n<p>flumina constiterint acuto?</p>\\n<p>Melt the cold by laying large heaps </p>\\n<p>Dissolve frigus ligna super foco </p>\\n<p>of firewood on the hearth. And be more generous</p>\\n<p>large reponens atque benignius</p>\\n<p>in bringing out the four-year old wine </p>\\n<p>deprome quadrimum Sabina,</p>\\n<p>Thaliarchus, in the Sabine two-handled wine-jar.</p>\\n<p>O Thaliarche, merum diota.</p>\\n<p>Leave the rest to the gods. Once they</p>\\n<p>Permitte divis cetera, qui simul </p>\\n<p>have calmed the winds battling it out</p>\\n<p>strauere ventos aequore fervido</p>\\n<p>on the turbulent sea, the cypresses</p>\\n<p>deproeliantis, nec cupressi</p>\\n<p>and the old ash trees will also be at peace.</p>\\n<p>nec veteres agitantur orni.</p>\\n<p>Stop investigating what will happen tomorrow, and</p>\\n<p>Quid sit futurum cras, fuge quaerere, et </p>\\n<p>whatever sort of day fortune gives, record it</p>\\n<p>quem fors dierum cumque dabit, lucro</p>\\n<p>as a windfall profit. Don’t dismiss </p>\\n<p>adpone nec dulcis amores </p>\\n<p>the delights of love or the dance-floor,</p>\\n<p>sperne, puer, neque tu choreas, </p>\\n<p>while you are young and you have no crabbed </p>\\n<p>donec virenti canities abest </p>\\n<p>white hairs. Seek out now at the agreed time </p>\\n<p>morosa. Nunc et Campus et areae</p>\\n<p>the main square and the piazzas </p>\\n<p>lenesque sub noctem susurri </p>\\n<p>and the gentle nighttime whispers.</p>\\n<p>composita repetantur hora,</p>\\n<p>Now is also the time for the girl’s pleasing laughter</p>\\n<p>nunc et latentis proditor intimo </p>\\n<p>from a distant corner, betraying where’s she’s hidden, </p>\\n<p>gratus puellae risus ab angulo</p>\\n<p>and a love-token, pulled off a forearm, </p>\\n<p>pignusque dereptum lacertis</p>\\n<p>or off a barely resisting finger. </p>\\n<p>Aut digito male pertinaci.</p>\\n<p><strong><em>First Thoughts</em></strong></p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (8).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 8&gt;</span>To begin with, some\\n  explanations\\n  and comments on the translation. In the first\\n  stanza, Soracte is a mountain in Italy. Horace describes the trees covered by a blanket of snow as if they were\\n  porters, struggling and failing to carry too heavy a load. So one visual image that comes to mind is of tree branches,\\n  overloaded with snow, breaking and falling to the ground; and this is what one Chinese translation of the poem says.\\n  But it is not what Horace says; and I think this sort of translation is doing some of the work that the reader is\\n  supposed to do. In general, I have restricted the Chinese to what Horace actually says. On another translation point,\\n  I have tried not to smooth over the strangeness of what Horace says. Sometimes the strangeness reflects the fact that\\n  Horace’s world is different from today’s. This fact is worth noticing and the strangeness brings these differences to\\n  your attention. Sometimes Horace’s language is deliberately strange. My Chinese teacher will tell me that in Chinese\\n  trees can’t feel tired, and she’s right. But in Latin trees can’t feel tired either, yet that’s what Horace says, so\\n  I’ve kept the strangeness of the language in Chinese.</p>\\n<p> </p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (9).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 9&gt;</span>In the second stanza, the name\\n  Thaliarchus is Greek. When a group of friends\\n  meet for drinks, the thaliarch is the person in charge of the drinking. Sabina is a region in northern Italy. Its wine\\n  was not the equivalent of an expensive French variety, more the sort of low-priced wine you’d buy at the local\\n  supermarket. Horace was a proponent of the simple life and he was disdainful of imported, foreign luxuries. Now, a\\n  general point here: when I say “Horace”, I usually mean Horace the poet, i.e. the picture of him that emerges from\\n  what he says in his poetry. What the real, historical Horace believed, it is difficult to say with certainty. What\\n  Horace the poet says was public speech for Horace the real person, so the relationship between the two Horaces is\\n  complicated. The word for wine jar is a Greek word, meaning two-eared, which refers to the two handles for carrying\\n  the jar. It stands out and sounds sophisticated, like when someone inserts a French word into a passage in English.\\n</p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (10).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 10&gt;</span>In the next two stanza,\\n  Horace\\n  gives some general and some specific advice to\\n  Thaliarchus. The darkest hour comes before the dawn; the future is unpredictable; count your blessings. While you’re\\n  still young, don’t miss out on romance and dancing. Horace’s advice uses accounting terminology: if you were to record\\n  what each day brings as you do household financial transactions, you would see that the gains outweigh the losses. It\\n  is not quite clear what dancing refers to. It is not couples dancing. Nor is it the dancing of flute girls, the\\n  ancient world’s version of exotic or pole dancing; for that did not take place outdoors in public spaces. Most likely\\n  it is a group of men doing formation dancing. White hairs are crabbed because people fret about getting white hairs,\\n  but also because white hairs are a sign of old age, and old people are crabbed. </p>\\n<p><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (11).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 11&gt;</span>The last two stanzas present\\n  a\\n  love scene. The Campus Martius was the main\\n  square in Rome. A young man is chasing a young girl. She finds a hiding place to escape from him, but laughs when he\\n  cannot find her. This reveals her hiding place, and he manages to snatch a love token from her, either a bracelet or a\\n  ring. Up until now, what Horace has been saying has been pretty straightforward, but now words start to have more than\\n  one meaning and the translation falls short. The laughter is “gratus” because it is a lovely sound, but also something\\n  to be grateful for, because it reveals where the girl is hiding; the corner is “intimo” because it is far away from\\n  anywhere else, but also because it brings the lovers close together; and the finger is “male pertinaci” because it\\n  barely resists and mischievously pretends to resist. </p>\\n<p>With this background, read the translation through a few times more. Write down your first impressions. What is the\\n  poem about? How does the narrative progress and how does the emotional tone change during the poem? Once you’ve\\n  written down your ideas, then proceed with the rest of the introduction. </p>\\n<p>It is easy to see what is attractive about the poem. First, there are the three marvellously described scenes –\\n  gazing at the countryside in winter, drinking wine by the fire and playing hide and seek with a pretty girl. Every\\n  detail of the three scenes is carefully chosen. Despite the gap of two millenia, Horace is describing sights and\\n  experiences that everyone today recognises. Snow still sits on tree branches in winter; it still feels comfortable to\\n  be dry indoors while the storm beats the rain against the window outside; and a romantic evening is still about\\n  chasing someone who wants to be found, even if love-tokens are no longer part of the modern dating scene. However, the\\n  poem also seems to contain some less successful elements. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (12).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 12&gt;</span>It starts with a\\n  cold day in the countryside, shifts indoors into a warm house and then moves outside again to a pleasant Spring or\\n  Summer evening in the city. That’s a lot of costume changes for one poem! What’s the logic behind them? The imagery,\\n  too, seems contradictory. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (13).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh\\n    13&gt;</span>Consider the two images Horace introduces to support\\n  his advice to leave things to the gods – the storm at sea and on the land. First, as the commentators have pointed\\n  out, there is the mismatch with the winter scene in the opening stanza. Both scenes symbolise unpleasant situations.\\n  But in the first everything is stationary – the mountain, the trees and the rivers ; in the second the wind is\\n  buffeting the trees and churning the sea. Secondly, there is the change from land to sea and back to land again. While\\n  each scene is successful on its own, it’s not clear how they fit together. </p>\\n<p>Another complaint relates to Horace’s advice. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (14).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh\\n    14&gt;</span> For starters, a modern reader\\n  finds it strange that a poem contains such explicit moralising. Surely a poem’s message is something that is implied\\n  or hinted at? But Horace just comes out and tells you what to think. Not only that, but some of the advice, given a\\n  moment’s thought, sounds naive. Yes, it makes sense to stop worrying about things in the future you have no control\\n  over and just enjoy the things you can enjoy now. Perhaps it’s also true that just as no good experience can last\\n  forever, neither can any bad experience. But who thinks that every day is a good day? Who thinks the day you get fired\\n  from your job or, to take a Roman example, the day your family farm is expropriated and given to a legionary veteran\\n  is a day where the pluses outweigh the minuses? And yet this is what Horace us saying. A final complaint, less\\n  remarked on by the commentators, is the weakness of the ending. <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (15).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh 15&gt;</span>The final\\n  stanza describes a sequence of episodes in a romantic encounter and then just stops at an arbitrary point. As result,\\n  the poem’s ending seems to be incomplete and missing something. This inconclusiveness is only made worse by there\\n  being two options for what happened and what was snatched from where. The poem doesn’t so much come to a conclusion as\\n  stumble off with a question mark.</p>\\n<p>Are these justified criticisms of what is otherwise a very beautiful poem? The greatest classical scholar of the\\n  19<sup>th</sup> century and the greatest classical scholar of the 20<sup>th</sup> century thought so. They judged it a\\n  flawed jewel. They admired its language and imagery, but thought that the poem doesn’t form a coherent whole. Now I’m\\n  sure their understanding of Horace and literary taste is much better than mine ( or yours ), so the prosecution’s case\\n  is a strong one. However, the defence can call an expert witness to refute this assessment: Horace himself. He chose\\n  to publish the Soracte Ode in the collection of poems he claimed would win him poetic immortality, so he was obviously\\n  satisfied with it; and his judgement of Latin poetry must be superior to any scholar’s. Given this, it is worth\\n  investigating further whether these complaints are really flaws or just misunderstandings of Horace’s poetic technique\\n  or intentions. </p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Lens of Literary Tradition </em></strong></p>\\n<p>One approach to figuring out Horace’s intentions is to investigate the literary tradition he was working in. A modern\\n  poet is expected to create something unique and original based on personal experience; calling a work derivative is to\\n  assess it as second rate; and plagiarism is unethical behaviour. But this expectation is a product of the Romantic\\n  revolution in Western literature, bolstered more recently, no doubt, by the commercial considerations of copyright\\n  law. The Romans had neither. Cicero wrote all five books of his treatise on ethics, On Moral Ends, in a little over\\n  one month. He could only do this because he took all the content, often word for word, from Greek philosophical\\n  handbooks and translated it into Latin. One of Catullus’ most famous poems is largely a translation from a Greek poem\\n  by Sappho. The major literary revolution in the classical world was the Hellenistic one, which marked the transition\\n  of Greece from an oral to a written culture. One of its consequences was to tie poetry more explicitly to the poetic\\n  tradition. A feature of Hellenistic poetry was deliberate and erudite allusions to older poets, which the educated\\n  reader was expected to recognise and appreciate. So it is wrong to expect Horace’s odes to be a freeform composition,\\n  emerging from his life experiences. </p>\\n<p>So what sort of poetry was Horace’s odes? <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"4\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"/Quintus Horatius Flaccus/Exh/Exh (16).jpg\\\">&lt;Exh\\n    16&gt;</span>To answer this question, let’s\\n  return to the example used earlier – the Westerner reading a Jin Yong novel – but make it a bit more complicated – a\\n  Westerner watching an episode of Wulin Waizhuan. Now, apart from having better Chinese than mine, what would sort of\\n  knowledge would be needed to get the jokes? I would suggest two things. First, knowledge of the world depicted in\\n  Chinese martial arts literature, not just its typical characters and plots, but also the beliefs, customs and social\\n  structure of the era the action takes place in, namely the Ming Dynasty. Secondly, a knowledge of the modern world and\\n  how it works. A lot of the humour comes from the interplay between these two worlds, for example imagining advertising\\n  and TV quiz shows in Ming Dynasty China. A similar sort of interplay is central to Horace’s odes as well; for lyric\\n  poetry in Latin did not exist before Horace and his challenge in inventing it was to take the metres and poetic\\n  conventions from the Greek tradition and not just turn them into Latin, but make them say something relevant to the\\n  Roman world. This means that someone reading Horace today has a more difficult job than the Westerner watching My Own\\n  Swordsman. The latter has already acquired half of the algorithm needed to make sense of an episode: modern life in\\n  any big Western city is not so different from life in any big Chinese city. But the modern reader of Horace lacks\\n  knowledge not only of Greek lyric poetry but also of life in Rome in the late Republic and early Principate period.\\n</p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Lens of Lyric Poetry</em></strong></p>\\n<p>Even if you don’t understand much about Chinese martial arts literature or modern-day China, you can still enjoy\\n  watching an episode of Wulin Waizhuan. It is a comedy TV series; and all comedy depends on funny characters, funny\\n  situations and funny language. Another way to approach the Soracte ode is to treat it not as Latin poetry, but simply\\n  as poetry. Poetry conveys ideas and feelings by using certain techniques, such as a careful selection of words, metre\\n  and imagery. It is your job as the reader to be sensitive to these features of a poem; for they will help you towards\\n  a better understanding and appreciation. </p>\\n<p>One aspect of a poem’s words is their sounds. Poetry relies, much more than prose, on the sound of words to convey\\n  meaning. Reading today is a silent and solitary activity, but this is a modern practice and it assumes the\\n  availability of mass-produced books. Roman poetry was written to be read aloud to an audience. Read the Latin version\\n  of Horace’s poem out loud; or, if you can’t read Latin, here’s a recording. </p>\\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"6\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"https://learningguide-1380131816.cos.ap-hongkong.myqcloud.com/mvp/other/Horace%20Recording.m4a\\\">\\n    &lt;Exh 17&gt;\\n  </span></p>\\n<p>\\n  Listen to the sounds of the words and notice the effect they have on you, even if you don’t know what the words mean.\\n  Now listen again to the first stanza, which talks about the trees struggling under the weight of the snow on their\\n  branches and the rivers turned into blocks of ice.\\n</p>\\n<p>\\n  How do the sounds of the Latin contribute to Horace’s description?\\n</p>\\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\\n  <span class=\\\"instance-node\\\" data-instance-type=\\\"3\\\"\\n    data-instance-content=\\\"How do the sounds of the Latin contribute to Horace’s description?\\\"\\n    data-instance-answer-type=\\\"input\\\"\\n    data-instance-answer-result=\\\"Can you hear the trees gasping, groaning and grunting under their burden in the sentence “nec iam sustineant onus/ silvae laborantes”? All those “S”, “M”, “N” and “T” sounds. Or in the last line “flumina constiterint acuto”, can you hear how the flowing sound of the Latin word for “river” (flumina) is followed by the repeated “C” and “T” sounds of words for “congeal” and “sharp” (constiterint and acuto). Compare this with the softness of the sounds in the line towards the end of the poem that describes the lovers’ nighttime meetings “lenesque sub noctem susurri”.\\\">\\n    Answer the question\\n  </span>\\n</p>\",\"nodes\":[]}]}]}],\"createdAt\":\"2026-09-16T02:07:17.811Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-09-16T02:46:13.432Z\"}") as ProductCourse;

export function apply(orm: MigrationOrm, _ctx: DataMigrationContext): DataChange[] {
  const courses = orm.table<ProductCourse>("courses");
  if (courses.find((item) => item.id === "quintus-horatius-flaccus" || item.slug === "quintus-horatius-flaccus").length) {
    return [{ action: "skip", kind: "course", id: "quintus-horatius-flaccus", reason: "exists" }];
  }
  if (!course) {
    return [{ action: "skip", kind: "course", id: "quintus-horatius-flaccus", reason: "missing-from-snapshot" }];
  }
  courses.insert(course);
  return [{ action: "add", kind: "course", id: course.id }];
}
